{"name":"Whitewash Oak","hex":"#cac9c0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"whitewash","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mixture of a powdered mineral substance (often slaked lime (containing calcium hydroxide), chalk (calcium carbonate), or both) and water which is used for painting surfaces such as fences and walls bright white.","A thing used to hide mistakes, or to create an appearance of honesty, propriety, etc.; also , an act of hiding mistakes or creating an appearance of honesty, propriety, etc.; especially, a campaign, investigation, etc., which intentionally hides or overlooks unfavourable facts."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To paint (a building, a wall, etc.) a bright white with whitewash.","To paint (a building, a wall, etc.) a bright white with whitewash."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/whitewash"},{"term":"oak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Noakhali."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deciduous tree with distinctive deeply lobed leaves, acorns, and notably strong wood, typically of England and northeastern North America, included in genus Quercus.","The wood of the oak."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oak"}],"entries":[{"title":"Whitewash","blocks":[{"text":"Whitewash, calcimine, kalsomine, calsomine, asbestis or lime paint is a type of paint made from slaked lime (calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)2) or chalk (calcium carbonate, CaCO3), sometimes known as \"whiting\". Various other additives are sometimes used."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Yarloop_wkshop_gnangarra_13.jpg/330px-Yarloop_wkshop_gnangarra_13.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitewash"},{"title":"Oak","blocks":[{"text":"An oak is a hardwood tree or shrub in the genus Quercus of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere, with some 475 species, both deciduous and evergreen. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle Eocene. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into Old World and New World clades, but many oak species hybridise freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Quercus_robur.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Nicolae_Ionescu_-_The_Whitewashers%2C_1928.jpg/500px-Nicolae_Ionescu_-_The_Whitewashers%2C_1928.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Nicolae_Ionescu_-_The_Whitewashers%2C_1928.jpg/500px-Nicolae_Ionescu_-_The_Whitewashers%2C_1928.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Whitewashers, photographed by Nicolae Ionescu [cs], 1928","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANicolae_Ionescu_-_The_Whitewashers%2C_1928.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Campsite-Oludeniz.JPG/500px-Campsite-Oludeniz.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Campsite-Oludeniz.JPG/1280px-Campsite-Oludeniz.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Campsite-Oludeniz.JPG/500px-Campsite-Oludeniz.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"White-painted trees in Turkey","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACampsite-Oludeniz.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Verloren_Vlei_Heritag